A promising native family is the paper’s own correspondence-colouring object, not ordinary colouring: a \(q\)-fold cover of \(K_{d,d}\), with every cross-edge represented by a perfect matching. I can sample a transversal first and condition every matching to avoid it, while keeping every candidate colour’s degree identical. The paper also explicitly warns that its sufficient conditions do not yield polynomial-time correspondence-colouring algorithms, so this is potentially Track A rather than a disguised polynomial task. I’m stress-testing that distribution now; if exact propagation or a construction-aware relaxation recovers the plant, I’ll discard it.
