1163:    By the Ramsey number upper bound of Erd\H{o}s and Szekeres~\cite{ErSz35}, in any~$S\subseteq V(G)$ such that $|S|\ge n/{(\log n)}^2$, there is guaranteed to be an independent set of~$G$ of size~$q$, for some~$q$ satisfying $q=(\frac12 +o(1)) \log_2 n$ as $n\to\infty$.
1165:    Repeat as follows for as long as possible: for each colour~$x$ that appears on at least $n/{(\log n)}^2$ lists, colour all the vertices of some independent set of size $q$ with colour $x$, and then remove~$x$ from all other lists. Afterwards, every colour appears on fewer than~$\lceil n/{(\log n)}^2\rceil$ lists, while every list has at least $\lceil n/{(\log n)}^2\rceil$ colours. Thus an application of Hall's theorem yields a proper $L$-colouring of $G$, as desired.
